The '''2021 EFL Cup final''' was the final of the [[2020–21 EFL Cup]]. It was played at [[Wembley Stadium]] in London, England, on 25 April 2021, between [[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]] and [[Tottenham Hotspur F.C.|Tottenham Hotspur]],<ref name="final">{{cite web|url=https://www.efl.com/news/2020/december/carabao-cup-final-to-be-rescheduled/|title=Carabao Cup Final to be rescheduled|publisher=English Football League|date=21 December 2020|access-date=22 December 2020|archive-date=21 December 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201221183701/https://www.efl.com/news/2020/december/carabao-cup-final-to-be-rescheduled/|url-status=live}}</ref> having been originally scheduled to take place on 28 February 2021.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.efl.com/news/2020/august/202021-season-dates-confirmed-across-the-efl/|title=2020/21 season dates confirmed across the EFL|publisher=English Football League|date=13 August 2020|access-date=13 August 2020|archive-date=18 August 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200818090732/https://www.efl.com/news/2020/august/202021-season-dates-confirmed-across-the-efl/|url-status=live}}</ref> The EFL's desire for a greater number of fans to be able to attend the match was the primary reason for the change of date.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/55403324|title=EFL Cup final moved from February to April|work=BBC Sport|date=21 December 2020|access-date=21 December 2020|archive-date=21 December 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201221220818/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/55403324|url-status=live}}</ref> It was the 61st EFL Cup final and the 14th to be played at the rebuilt Wembley Stadium.<ref>{{cite web |first=James M. |last=Ross |title=England - Football League Cup Finals |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablese/engleagcuphist.html |website=Rec.Sport.Soccer Statistics Foundation |date=2 April 2020 |access-date=25 April 2021 |archive-date=29 June 2012 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120629080535/http://www.rsssf.com/tablese/engleagcuphist.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

The match was the ninth Football League/EFL Cup final to be played by Tottenham, of which four have ended in victory. One of said wins was the club's most recent domestic trophy up to that point, occurring in [[2008 Football League Cup final|2008]].

Manchester City appeared in their ninth Football League/EFL Cup final, and their fourth consecutive final, becoming only the second team to do so, after [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] between 1981 and 1984. City's win equalled Liverpool's records of eight EFL Cup wins and four consecutive wins. City's manager [[Pep Guardiola]] became the first coach to win the cup in four consecutive years (the first three of Liverpool's four consecutive titles were won by [[Bob Paisley]], and the fourth by [[Joe Fagan]]).

The match was televised live by [[Sky Sports]], while radio commentary was provided by [[BBC Radio]] and [[Talksport]].

==Ticketing==
On 22 February 2021, Prime Minister [[Boris Johnson]] announced that football supporters would not be permitted in stadiums until 17 May 2021 and then only with a reduced stadium capacity.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.manchestereveningnews.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/fans-carabao-cup-final-wembley-19892605|title=EFL hoping fans can attend Man City vs Tottenham Carabao Cup final|first=Chris|last=Watson|date=February 22, 2021|website=Manchester Evening News|access-date=February 23, 2021|archive-date=February 23, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210223002122/https://www.manchestereveningnews.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/fans-carabao-cup-final-wembley-19892605|url-status=live}}</ref> On 4 April, in yet another schedule to open events to fans, a plan to allow 8,000 supporters at the final was announced. The final was also planned be a pilot event for [[COVID passports in the United Kingdom|COVID passports]].<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/56630189|title=Carabao Cup final to have 8,000 fans|work=BBC Sport |date=4 April 2021|access-date=4 April 2021|archive-date=4 April 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210404082544/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/56630189|url-status=live}}</ref>

In a further announcement on 13 April, a plan to allow 2,000 fans for each club was put forward. This would be subject to the testing of fans for COVID-19 with both a [[lateral flow test]] and a [[Polymerase chain reaction|PCR test]] before the game and a PCR test after the game and would not permit those under 18, those deemed clinically vulnerable or pregnant women to attend.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/56732740|title=2,000 Spurs & Man City fans at final|work=BBC Sport |date=13 April 2021|access-date=13 April 2021|archive-date=13 April 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210413105144/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/56732740|url-status=live}}</ref> Level Playing Field, a charity which campaigns for disabled rights, and a Tottenham Hotspur disabled fans' group both voiced their disapproval that a sub-section of society were excluded from attending the final.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.sportsmole.co.uk/football/spurs/league-cup/news/tottenham-and-man-city-fans-unite-against-efl-cup-final-ticket-allocation_443072.html|title=Tottenham and Man City fans unite against EFL Cup final ticket allocation - Sports Mole|website=amp.sportsmole.co.uk|access-date=14 April 2021|date=13 April 2021|archive-date=14 April 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210414025124/https://amp.sportsmole.co.uk/football/spurs/league-cup/news/tottenham-and-man-city-fans-unite-against-efl-cup-final-ticket-allocation_443072.html?newsnow|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.sportsmole.co.uk/football/league-cup/news/tottenham-disabled-fans-group-hits-out-at-discrimination-over-vulnerable-fans_443004.html|title=Tottenham disabled fans' group hits out at "discrimination" over vulnerable fans - Sports Mole|website=amp.sportsmole.co.uk|date=13 April 2021|access-date=14 April 2021|archive-date=14 April 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210414063358/https://amp.sportsmole.co.uk/football/league-cup/news/tottenham-disabled-fans-group-hits-out-at-discrimination-over-vulnerable-fans_443004.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

