Manchester City, as a Premier League club involved in the [[2020–21 UEFA Champions League]], started the competition in the third round. They beat [[EFL Championship]] side [[AFC Bournemouth|Bournemouth]] who were managed by [[Jason Tindall]] at the time and won 2–1 on what would their only home match in the tournament with goals from [[Liam Delap]] and [[Phil Foden]] for Manchester City and one goal from [[Sam Surridge]] for Bournemouth.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/54190674|title=Manchester City 2-1 Bournemouth: Liam Delap delighted with 'dream' debut|date=24 September 2020|access-date=6 January 2021|work=BBC Sport|archive-date=28 September 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200928192617/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/54190674|url-status=live}}</ref>

In the fourth round they defeated fellow Premier League team [[Burnley F.C.|Burnley]] 3–0 at [[Turf Moor]] with two goals from [[Raheem Sterling]] and one from [[Ferran Torres]].<ref>{{cite web|last=Johnston|first=Neil|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/54298224|title=Burnley 0-3 Manchester City: Raheem Sterling scores twice in Carabao Cup|date=30 September 2020|access-date=6 January 2021|work=BBC Sport|archive-date=24 October 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201024063758/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/54298224|url-status=live}}</ref>

In the quarter-finals they defeated [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]] 4–1 at the [[Emirates Stadium]] with one goal each for City from [[Gabriel Jesus]], [[Riyad Mahrez]], Foden and [[Aymeric Laporte]] and one goal for Arsenal from [[Alexandre Lacazette]]. This match also saw the face-off between [[List of Arsenal F.C. managers|Arsenal manager]] [[Mikel Arteta]] and [[List of Manchester City F.C. managers|Manchester City manager]] [[Pep Guardiola]] as Arteta worked under Guardiola before becoming Arsenal manager.<ref>{{cite web|last=Johnston|first=Neil|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/55312316|title=Arsenal 1-4 Manchester City: Pep Guardiola triumphs in battle with former assistant Mikel Arteta|date=22 December 2020|access-date=6 January 2021|work=BBC Sport|archive-date=7 January 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210107234349/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/55312316|url-status=live}}</ref>

In the semi-finals they defeated [[Manchester derby|local rivals]] [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] 2–0 at [[Old Trafford]] with one goal each from Manchester City [[Captain (association football)|captain]] [[Fernandinho (footballer, born May 1985)|Fernandinho]] and [[John Stones]]. The semi-finals were played over one-leg instead over the traditional two-legs in order to reduce fixture congestion.<ref>{{cite news|title=Man Utd 0-2 Man City: Pep Guardiola's side reach Carabao Cup final|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/55481278|work=BBC Sport|first=Simon|last=Stone|date=6 January 2021|access-date=6 January 2021|archive-date=5 January 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210105233021/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/55481278|url-status=live}}</ref>

